@prefix mqtt: <http://example.org/agora/mqtt#> .
@prefix schema: <https://schema.org/> .
@prefix unit: <http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/> .
@prefix ag:   <http://example.org/agora#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix dht11: <http://example.org/agora/dht11#> .
@prefix wokwi: <http://example.org/agora/wokwi#> .
@prefix onewire: <http://example.org/agora/onewire#> .
@prefix mc: <http://example.org/agora/microcontroller#> .
@prefix perception: <http://example.org/agora/perception#> .
@prefix ssn-system: <http://www.w3.org/ns/ssn/systems/> .
@prefix ssn: <http://www.w3.org/ns/ssn/> .
@prefix sosa: <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/> .

<http://example.org/agora/dht11> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "DHT11 temperature and humidity sensor" ;
    rdfs:comment """One part, its own package, layered on the microcontroller vocabulary and on one-wire. This is the shape every concrete peripheral should take: what it IS, how many legs it has and what they are for, and the shapes that refuse it wired wrong. Adding a part is adding a directory.""" .

dht11:TempHumidityPeripheral a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf mc:Peripheral ;
    rdfs:label "Temperature and humidity sensor" ;
    rdfs:comment """The family. One device reporting TWO properties down one line, which is what made it awkward: a sensor observes one property, so a device like this is TWO sensors sharing one reading topic, and each says which value in the message is its own with mqtt:readingPointer. Closed #51; see knowledge/decisions/a-reading-is-one-value-so-it-is-pointed-at.md.

**One message, because one measurement.** This used to be explained as a transport economy — *the board publishes once because it is one MQTT client with one credential* — and that is true and is not the reason. It would justify batching two unrelated readings into one message, which is exactly what the values in here are NOT. The part answers a single request with a single frame containing both values, produced by the same sampling; it cannot be asked for temperature alone. So the reading arrives together because it was TAKEN together, and the credential merely means nothing has to undo that.

The difference is not academic — it is what the reading path got wrong. Believing the transport reason, each value was stamped as it was written, seconds of code apart, and the series store recorded a 25 ms difference between two halves of one physical read. Believing the physical one, a message is a measurement and carries one instant.""" .

dht11:Dht11 a owl:Class ;
    mc:modelName "KY-015 (DHT11)" ; rdfs:subClassOf dht11:TempHumidityPeripheral ; rdfs:label "DHT11" ;
    wokwi:part "wokwi-dht22" ;
    wokwi:pin [ mc:pinRole onewire:DataPinRole ; wokwi:name "SDA" ] ,
              [ mc:pinRole mc:PowerPinRole     ; wokwi:name "VCC" ] ,
              [ mc:pinRole mc:GroundPinRole    ; wokwi:name "GND" ] ;
    rdfs:comment "Three legs and a pull-up to its own VCC, which is the fact worth writing down: the data line idles at whatever the part is powered from, so the rail choice IS the logic level. On a KY-015 breakout the pull-up is on the board. Rated 3-5V, and that range is a trap next to a 3.3V microcontroller — it will run perfectly happily on 5V and present 5V to an input that is not 5V tolerant." .

#################  What it is made of  #################
#
# The DHT11 is the worked example for describing a part, and this is the section to copy. W3C's
# SSN primer describes exactly this device — `tests/fixtures/w3c-ssn/dht22.ttl`, held byte-identical
# — and the one structural difference between theirs and ours is the whole lesson:
#
#   THEIRS  <DHT22/4578> a ssn:System ; ssn:hasSubSystem <DHT22/4578#TemperatureSensor> .
#   OURS    dht11:Dht11 rdfs:subClassOf [ owl:onProperty ssn:hasSubSystem ; owl:someValuesFrom … ]
#
# They describe ONE UNIT, serial 4578, and every fact is a fact about that unit. We ship a
# vocabulary for a MODEL, and the units are named by a world. Their form is right for a data set
# and wrong for a package: a second DHT11 would repeat all of it. So every fact here that a
# datasheet states is stated on the class, once — and reaches units by entailment, which is what
# `owl:Restriction` is for and what a bare class-level triple never does. See
# knowledge/decisions/what-is-true-of-a-part-is-true-of-every-one-of-them.md.
#
# TWO SENSORS, because a `sosa:Sensor` observes ONE property and this part reports two. They are
# `ssn:hasSubSystem` of the part and not two loose sensors on the board, which is the fact that
# makes the shared reading topic and the shared instant follow rather than need explaining twice.

# NAMED FOR WHAT THEY ARE. These were `dht11:TemperatureChannel` and `dht11:HumidityChannel`
# for exactly as long as it took someone to ask why, given the class is `rdfs:subClassOf
# sosa:Sensor`. Two reasons the standard word wins, and they are the general rule for this
# repository rather than a fact about DHTs:
#
#   - W3C's own description of this device family calls them `#TemperatureSensor` and
#     `#HumiditySensor`. Where the standard has a word, use it.
#   - "channel" was already taken. The README uses it for an MQTT topic, and the RGB LED has
#     three of them meaning colours. A word doing three jobs in one repository does none.
#
# It leaves `dht11:TempHumidityPeripheral` — which is `rdfs:subClassOf mc:Peripheral` and was
# called `…Sensor` — as the same mistake in the other direction: the one thing here that is NOT
# a `sosa:Sensor` was the one wearing the name. Its `rdfs:label` is still "Temperature and
# humidity sensor", because that is what it is called on the shelf. A label may be colloquial;
# an IRI is what a query has to be right about.

dht11:TemperatureSensor a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf sosa:Sensor , ssn:System ;
    rdfs:label "DHT11 temperature sensor" ;
    rdfs:comment "The thermistor half. A sensor rather than a part: it has no legs of its own, cannot be bought, and cannot be read on its own — it is one of the two things the frame carries. What it OBSERVES is deliberately not stated here; see the note below on why a part vocabulary does not name a domain property." .

dht11:HumiditySensor a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf sosa:Sensor , ssn:System ;
    rdfs:label "DHT11 humidity sensor" ;
    rdfs:comment "The capacitive half, and the reason the part drifts: the humidity element ages and is the first thing to go out of calibration, while the thermistor stays honest. Worth separating for that alone — a recalibration applies to one of the two and not to the part." .

# `owl:someValuesFrom` and NOT `owl:hasValue`, and the difference is not stylistic. A capability
# is ONE individual every DHT11 shares — the datasheet's number is the same number — so
# `hasValue` names it and rule 5 materialises it. A sub-sensor is NOT shared: this DHT11's
# thermistor is not that one's, so what the class can say is that every unit has SOME
# temperature sensor, and the individual is minted by whoever names the unit.
#
# That is why nothing here entails a sub-sensor into being, and it is not a gap. An existential
# would need a fresh individual per unit — skolemisation, which `agent/inference.py`
# deliberately does not do. The world names the two; this says what they must be, and
# `shapes.ttl` is what refuses a unit that lacks one.
dht11:Dht11 rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty ssn:hasSubSystem ; owl:someValuesFrom dht11:TemperatureSensor ] ,
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty ssn:hasSubSystem ; owl:someValuesFrom dht11:HumiditySensor ] .

#################  What it does  #################
#
# Procedures at THREE levels of the same part, which is the reason to state them rather than
# leave them implied by the wiring: each answers a different question.
#
#   onewire:Transaction   HOW it is talked to        — the protocol package's, referenced not copied
#   dht11:CombinedRead    what the PART does         — one frame, both values, one instant
#   dht11:TemperatureRead what a SUB-SENSOR contributes — the half of the frame that is its own
#
# None is a sense mode. `perception:senseMode` answers *who holds the clock* and carries
# `sh:maxCount 1`; these answer *what happens when the moment arrives*, and a system may
# implement as many as it can perform. That is why `ssn:implements` is the right predicate here
# and the wrong one there — see the note in perception's ontology.

dht11:CombinedRead a sosa:Procedure ; rdfs:label "Combined temperature and humidity read" ;
    rdfs:comment "One request, two values, one instant. The part samples both and clocks them out in a single 40-bit frame — humidity, temperature, checksum — so neither can be obtained without the other and the two are simultaneous BY CONSTRUCTION. Belongs to the whole part and not to either sensor, which is the shape of the claim: the sub-systems each observe one property, and what the part adds is that it produces them together. The library our firmware uses makes the same statement in code — a second call inside the part's stated Frequency is served from the buffer the first filled, never from the wire — so the firmware's two calls have always been one conversation, whatever the reading path did with them afterwards." .

dht11:TemperatureRead a sosa:Procedure ; rdfs:label "DHT11 temperature read" ;
    rdfs:comment "Bytes 2 and 3 of the frame. Named separately from the combined read because an Observation is made BY a sensor and each half IS one — so `sosa:usedProcedure` on a temperature reading should name what produced that value, not the conversation it arrived in. It cannot be performed alone: obtaining it IS performing dht11:CombinedRead, and this names the part of that which is this sensor's." .

dht11:HumidityRead a sosa:Procedure ; rdfs:label "DHT11 humidity read" ;
    rdfs:comment "Bytes 0 and 1 of the frame, and the same standing as the temperature read: a name for one sensor's share of one conversation, not a second conversation." .

# Restrictions rather than the bare triples this replaced. `dht11:Dht11 ssn:implements
# dht11:CombinedRead` is punning — it says the CLASS implements it, and entailed nothing about
# any device: on the world this repository ships, the only subject of `ssn:implements` was
# `dht11:Dht11` itself, so no sensor in it had ever been said to do anything. Same defect the
# capability had, found the same way, fixed the same way.
dht11:Dht11 rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty ssn:implements ; owl:hasValue dht11:CombinedRead ] ,
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty ssn:implements ; owl:hasValue onewire:Transaction ] .

dht11:TemperatureSensor rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty ssn:implements ; owl:hasValue dht11:TemperatureRead ] .

dht11:HumiditySensor rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty ssn:implements ; owl:hasValue dht11:HumidityRead ] .

#################  What it can honour  #################
#
# A datasheet fact, and therefore on the CLASS: every DHT11 has it, unlike `probe:rawDry` which
# is measured per probe in the pot it sits in. A device that reports slowly is not a device
# wired badly, so this is not a shape — it is what the part IS.
#
# `ssn-system:Frequency` is SSN's term, and its definition is what makes it the right one:
# "The smallest possible time between one Observation, Actuation, or Sampling and the next,
# under the defined Conditions." A time and not a rate, so it reads as a floor.
#
# TWO seconds because that is the part's specified sampling period — it will return the
# previous reading if asked sooner. Below the society's own floor of ten, so it constrains
# nothing here today, and that is worth stating rather than hiding: the mechanism binds when a
# device is slower than the constitution, and none of the hardware in this repository is.
# Stated ONCE, and reaching instances by entailment rather than by repetition. The capability is
# named, because `owl:hasValue` takes an individual and a blank node is not one; and the class is
# put UNDER a restriction rather than being handed the property itself. The difference is the
# whole of this: `dht11:Dht11 ssn-system:hasSystemCapability …` is punning — legal RDF, and it
# says the CLASS has the capability, which entails nothing whatever about a DHT11 you can hold.
# The restriction says every instance does, which is what a datasheet means.
#
# `agent/inference.py` materialises it (rule 5), so the sovereign — which alone loads both the
# vocabulary and the wiring — observes `ag:air_sensor_fern ssn-system:hasSystemCapability
# dht11:ContinuousOperationCapability` without anyone having written it down. See
# knowledge/decisions/what-is-true-of-a-part-is-true-of-every-one-of-them.md.
dht11:Dht11 rdfs:subClassOf [
    a owl:Restriction ;
    owl:onProperty ssn-system:hasSystemCapability ;
    owl:hasValue  dht11:ContinuousOperationCapability ] .

# Named for its CONDITION and not for the part, because the condition is the axis a second one
# would differ along. SSN puts "under the defined Conditions" in the definition of Frequency
# itself, so a part that could be read faster on mains than on a battery states two of these
# rather than one number with a caveat — and the two would then need telling apart by name.
# Nothing here is battery-powered yet; see issue #78.
dht11:ContinuousOperationCapability a ssn-system:SystemCapability ;
    rdfs:label "DHT11 sampling capability, in continuous operation" ;
    ssn-system:inCondition [ a ssn-system:Condition ; rdfs:label "continuous operation" ] ;
    ssn-system:hasSystemProperty [ a ssn-system:Frequency , schema:PropertyValue ; schema:value 2 ; schema:unitCode unit:SEC ] .

#################  What each sensor measures, and what it will not say  #################
#
# Accuracy, resolution and range are per SENSOR, while the sampling floor above is the PART's,
# and that split is physical rather than tidy-minded: the floor is a property of the frame, which
# is one conversation carrying both values, whereas how finely the thermistor resolves has nothing
# to do with the humidity element. It is also why a world projects the floor onto both sensors
# and does not project these — an agent is handed the two sensors, and the floor is the one fact of the
# part's it must honour.
#
# `rdfs:seeAlso` carries the datasheet exactly as W3C's example does for the DHT22. Figures are
# Aosong's for the DHT11; the humidity span differs across revisions of this part (20-80 and
# 20-90 are both published), which is the honest reason `ssn-system:inCondition` is not
# decoration — a second capability under a second condition is how that gets said when it
# matters, and nothing here depends on it yet.

dht11:Dht11 rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.mouser.com/datasheet/2/758/DHT11-Technical-Data-Sheet-Translated-Version-1143054.pdf> .

dht11:TemperatureSensor rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty ssn-system:hasSystemCapability ;
      owl:hasValue dht11:TemperatureSensorCapability ] .

dht11:TemperatureSensorCapability a ssn-system:SystemCapability ;
    rdfs:label "DHT11 temperature sensor capability" ;
    ssn-system:inCondition [ a ssn-system:Condition ; rdfs:label "continuous operation" ] ;
    ssn-system:hasSystemProperty
        [ a ssn-system:MeasurementRange , schema:PropertyValue ;
          schema:minValue 0 ; schema:maxValue 50 ; schema:unitCode unit:DEG_C ] ,
        [ a ssn-system:Accuracy , schema:PropertyValue ;
          schema:minValue -2 ; schema:maxValue 2 ; schema:unitCode unit:DEG_C ] ,
        [ a ssn-system:Resolution , schema:PropertyValue ;
          schema:value 1 ; schema:unitCode unit:DEG_C ] .

dht11:HumiditySensor rdfs:subClassOf
    [ a owl:Restriction ; owl:onProperty ssn-system:hasSystemCapability ;
      owl:hasValue dht11:HumiditySensorCapability ] .

dht11:HumiditySensorCapability a ssn-system:SystemCapability ;
    rdfs:label "DHT11 humidity sensor capability" ;
    ssn-system:inCondition [ a ssn-system:Condition ; rdfs:label "continuous operation" ] ;
    ssn-system:hasSystemProperty
        [ a ssn-system:MeasurementRange , schema:PropertyValue ;
          schema:minValue 20 ; schema:maxValue 90 ; schema:unitCode unit:PERCENT ] ,
        [ a ssn-system:Accuracy , schema:PropertyValue ;
          schema:minValue -5 ; schema:maxValue 5 ; schema:unitCode unit:PERCENT ] ,
        [ a ssn-system:Resolution , schema:PropertyValue ;
          schema:value 1 ; schema:unitCode unit:PERCENT ] .

# WHAT IS ABSENT, deliberately: neither sensor states `sosa:observes`.
#
# It is tempting — "the temperature sensor observes air temperature" reads like a datasheet fact,
# and W3C's example is free to say it because it describes an installed unit. Ours cannot. The
# property individual would be `water:AirTemperature`, and `water:` is the DOMAIN vocabulary: v1
# is plant watering, and AGENTS.md is explicit that the domain is a plug-in and the plant/water
# language is the example rather than the architecture. A part package that imported it would make
# this thermistor unusable in a world that is not about plants, which is precisely backwards — the
# part is the domain-neutral thing here.
#
# So the world says what a sensor observes, and this says what one IS. The line is not
# "instance versus class"; it is "the part's own facts versus the installation's". A DHT11
# resolves to 1 degree in every world that will ever use one. What that degree is a temperature OF
# is not the part's business.
